  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Statistical capacity building plan prepared and implemented
Statistical master plan finalized
Functioning NSO in place
Key economic indicators published</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Inadequate availability of data</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Comprehensive institutional capacity needs and gap assessment of NSO staff and infrastructure (software and hardware) 
2. Supply of ICT equipment and accessories 
3. Workshop and training of NSO staff, including refresher training. Design and deliver tailored capacity building programmes (using appropriate methods and channels) to NSO staff including zoba and related ministries and departments. Capacity building trainings will cover statistical methodologies, international norms, standards and definitions, CPI and PPI theories, survey methodology, database, data entry, data analysis, data dissemination.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">As per the request of the Government, UNDP in collaboration with UNECA and UNICEF organized a first training on Voluntary National Review (VNR) that brought together 27 relevant government ministries, institutions, CSOs and UN agencies. The objective of the workshop was to help government partners on the preparation and submission of Eritrea first VNR and was very useful and informative. Methodology and process for preparation and presentation of the review were well presented. Eritrea&#x2019;s first VNR preparation would be very fundamental to identify progress towards SDG implementation and existing gaps in the implementation, the impacts of COVID-19, possible data gaps, and other relevant factors. Some countries&#x2019; best VNR practices were shared including making realistic roadmap/work plan, sufficient consultations with stakeholders, providing institutional arrangements, early drafting arrangements, timely preparation of the VNR write up, editing, and presentation. It was also noted that the VNR would be good practice to compose analysis, strengths and weaknesses as well as conveying the key findings, which include success stories, challenges, and areas for support. focusing on the approaches to VNR preparation and presentation. The workshop was participatory and has invigorated the relationship of the Government and the UNDP and the UN in general.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">N/A</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">N/A</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. Comprehensive institutional capacity needs and gap assessment of NSO staff and infrastructure (software and hardware) 
2. Supply of ICT equipment and accessories. 
3. Workshop and training of NSO staff, including refresher training. Design and deliver tailored capacity building programmes (using appropriate methods and technology) to NSO staff including zoba and related ministries and departments. Capacity building trainings will cover statistical methodologies, international norms, standards and definitions, preparation of CPI and PPI, survey methodology, database, data entry, data analysis, data dissemination.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">The supply of ICT equipment and accessories are in progress but the results are going to be achieved next year due to delay in signing the Joint Work Plan and implementation of the project. The UN Joint Work Plan was signed on 27 October 2022.

Eritrea was among the 11 countries that presented their VNR this year. As per the request of government, UNDP supported Eritrea to prepare and present its first VNR this year. The report contains the country&#x2019;s experiences, including successes, challenges, and key takeaways in the implementation of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development. The preparation process followed the whole &#x2013;of-government and whole-of-society approach which facilitated active participation and contribution of different ministries, departments, Civil Society Organizations (CSOs) including NUEYS, NUEW, and NCEW; academia, and United Nation Development System (UNDS) in Eritrea. Eritrea&#x2019;s VNR has focused on two SDGs: SDG3 on good health and well-being and SDG13 on climate action. In the area of SDG3, achievements include: increased universal health coverage with investments in expanding health infrastructure, medical equipment and medicine, and personnel; improved provision of child and maternal health services; significant reduction in childhood and maternal mortality; universal immunization coverage among eligible children; close to zero HIV infection and prevalence; significant reduction in the level of infection and deaths due to malaria leading towards malaria elimination and major inroads against tuberculosis, hepatitis B, and neglected tropical diseases. 
In the area of SDG13, the report highlighted significant achievements in reducing the impact of climate change through implementing of several climate change mitigation and adaptation measures. Measures taken by the government and people of Eritrea include expanding water and soil conservation programmes; establishment of enclosures and protected areas, establishment of vast network of terraces, dams and ponds; restoration of degraded land; enhancing use and promotion of renewable and clean energy alternatives. Other important practice shared to the participants was the summer holiday afforestation and environment management programme carried out by young people and community. Moreover, working for better partnership and cooperation with local and global development partners was pointed out to be crucial in moving forward to the successful implementation of the SDGs in the remaining period of the agenda.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
